# ===========================================================================
# COMPOSE_PROFILES - the ONE place this variable is set (stack-layers D15)
# ===========================================================================
# It is a SINGLE GLOBAL COMPOSE VARIABLE, not a per-plane one. Every plane
# driven with `--env-file .env` - anchor, inference, frontend, memory, search,
# coder - reads the SAME value, and each project simply ignores profile names
# it does not declare. So there is exactly one assignment in this file, here,
# and every plane's own section below points at it instead of assigning it
# again.
#
# WHY THAT MATTERS: a duplicate key in an env file is LAST-WINS, silently,
# exit 0 (measured against compose v5.3, 2026-09-19). Two blocks each telling
# you to "put this in your .env" is how a host ends up with the second one's
# value and loses the first one's planes, with nothing to say so.
#
# A `--profile` flag on the command line REPLACES this value for that
# invocation rather than adding to it (measured; sl-driver-parity). That is why
# it is SET here rather than passed: llm-gateway reads COMPOSE_PROFILES itself
# to decide which model groups to register, so a CLI flag alone would start the
# local backends without listing their models.
#
# EVERY PROFILE, BY PLANE:
#   inference  local        llama-cpp-upstream, llama-cpp-embed-upstream,
#                           llm-queue, lm-models-backup - the GPU backends.
#                           OFF = a LiteLLM gateway + ledger + Admin UI only,
#                           which is what a machine with no GPU runs.
#   frontend   stock        Open WebUI alone: pinned upstream image, no GPU, no
#                           local build, no tailscale, no other plane.
#              gpu          the CUDA local build + the nvidia device
#                           reservation + USE_CUDA/NVIDIA_* + the llm-net /
#                           app-net seams + the status-pipe mount. Needs an
#                           NVIDIA container runtime. Mutually exclusive with
#                           `stock`: both define container_name openwebui, and
#                           turning on both is refused by `docker compose
#                           config` before anything starts.
#              tailscale    the tailnet node welded to openwebui's netns, and
#                           its state backup. Usable ONLY together with `gpu`.
#
# NOT part of this value, deliberately:
#   portal     internet     passed on the command line by
#                           scripts/portal/portal-on.ps1. Exposing the stack
#                           stays a human action, so it must not be standing.
#   agent-org  workers, cloud   these projects load their OWN env files
#   ob1        idea-refinery    (agent-org/docker/.env, OB1/docker/.env), so
#                           their profiles never belong here. sl-ob1-profiles
#                           adds research/wiki/notebook THERE, not to this line.
#
# THE TWO CANONICAL VALUES:
#   a fresh clone .................. COMPOSE_PROFILES=stock
#   the operator's GPU deployment .. COMPOSE_PROFILES=local,gpu,tailscale
#
# `stock` is what ships here: .env.example is what a first-time clone copies
# and what the pre-commit compose check renders every project against, and the
# cloud-only inference render would be untestable with `local` on.
#
# ON A GPU HOST THE FULL VALUE IS REQUIRED, NOT ADVISORY - and it is the FULL
# one: `gpu,tailscale` alone drops `local` and takes the inference backends
# down. Without the frontend's profiles at all:
#   `up -d`   starts openwebui-backup and nothing else;
#   `down`    LEAVES openwebui and tailscale running (compose only tears down
#             services whose profile is active);
#   any verb NAMING tailscale (up/stop/start/restart/rm/ps/config, and
#             --no-deps too) exits 1 with `no such service: openwebui`, because
#             naming a service activates only ITS profile and tailscale's
#             network_mode/depends_on point at the gpu-profiled openwebui.
# So the watchdog's tailscale self-heal and emergency-recovery.ps1 are dead
# until the line is right, silently. Verify with
# scripts\checks\check-watchdog-repair-targets.ps1 after editing .env.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=stock

# Open WebUI image for the `stock` profile only (the `gpu` profile builds and
# uses openwebui:local). Uncomment to pin a different upstream release; the
# default below is what frontend/docker-compose.yml falls back to.
#OWUI_IMAGE=ghcr.io/open-webui/open-webui:v0.11.0


# GPU Device Mapping
# Use: nvidia-smi --query-gpu=index,name --format=csv
# Set by index (0, 1, ...) or GPU UUID (GPU-xxxxxxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxxxxxxxxxx)
GPU_LLAMA_CPP_DEVICE_ID=0
GPU_LLAMA_CPP_EMBED_DEVICE_ID=1
GPU_AISTACK_DEVICE_ID=1

# --- Inference plane: local backends ON/OFF + where the GGUFs live -----------
# (stack-layers sl-inference-split, 2026-09-19)
#
# The `local` profile turns on llama-cpp-upstream, llama-cpp-embed-upstream,
# llm-queue and lm-models-backup. With it OFF the inference project is a
# LiteLLM gateway + ledger + Admin UI only, which is what a machine with no GPU
# runs. THE OPERATOR'S DEPLOYMENT NEEDS IT ON.
#
# It is turned on through COMPOSE_PROFILES, and this file sets that variable in
# exactly ONE place - the section at the top. Do NOT add an assignment here:
# the variable is global and a duplicate key is last-wins, silently, so a
# second one would drop whatever the first turned on. Put `local` in the value
# up there, alongside the frontend's profiles:
#   COMPOSE_PROFILES=local,gpu,tailscale
# (SET, not passed as `--profile local`: llm-gateway reads COMPOSE_PROFILES
# itself to decide which model groups to register, so the CLI flag alone starts
# the local backends without listing their models.)
#
# Host directory holding the llama.cpp GGUF store, bind-mounted read-only into
# llama-cpp-upstream (/models) and lm-models-backup (/data). Use an ABSOLUTE
# path - a relative one resolves against inference/compose/, not the repo root.
# Compose default if unset: <repo>/data/models/gguf (deliberately a `:-` default
# and not a `:?` guard, so a cloud-only node does not have to set a variable
# only the profiled services use). The operator's current value:
LM_MODELS_DIR=C:\Users\yamao\.lmstudio\models

# llama.swap model defaults for llama-cpp service
# Select model via request model id: qwen36-35b-a3b or qwen36-27b
LLAMA_CPP_CACHE_TYPE_K=q4_0
LLAMA_CPP_CACHE_TYPE_V=q4_0
LLAMA_CPP_TIMEOUT=300

# Qwen 3.6 35B-A3B profile (currently active)
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_MODEL_PATH=/models/lmstudio-community/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-GGUF/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_CTX_SIZE=262144
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_N_GPU_LAYERS=99
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_N_PARALLEL=1
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_BATCH=1024
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_UBATCH=512
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_CACHE_TYPE_K=q4_0
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_CACHE_TYPE_V=q4_0
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_35B_REASONING_BUDGET=4096

# Qwen 3.6 27B profile (download model and adjust path if needed)
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_MODEL_PATH=/models/lmstudio-community/Qwen3.6-27B-GGUF/Qwen3.6-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_CTX_SIZE=262144
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_N_GPU_LAYERS=99
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_N_PARALLEL=1
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_BATCH=1024
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_UBATCH=512
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_CACHE_TYPE_K=q4_0
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_CACHE_TYPE_V=q4_0
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_REASONING_BUDGET=4096

# Mnemory should stay below the server context to preserve prompt/output headroom.
MNEMORY_LLM_CONTEXT_SIZE=131072

# key expires Aug 28 2025
# create a new one here: 
# https://login.tailscale.com/admin/settings/keys
TAILSCALE_AUTH_KEY=putyourtskeyhere


# Mnemory Memory Service
# API key for MCP authentication (single key)
MCP_API_KEY=your-mnemory-api-key-here
# JSON dict mapping keys to user IDs: {"api-key": "user-id"}
MCP_API_KEYS=
# Backup retention -- how many MOST-RECENT archives to keep (count, not days).
# Default 2 = ~2 nightly snapshots local; the weekly NAS sync gives you
# ~2 additional copies per NAS slot.
BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2
# No cron variable exists: mnemory-backup is a sleep-loop sidecar. Its
# schedule is MNEMORY_BACKUP_INTERVAL (seconds), under "Backup sidecar knobs"
# below (memory/docker-compose.yml:111).
# OpenWebUI backup retention count (default 2 most recent)
OPENWEBUI_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2
# No cron variable exists: openwebui-backup is a sleep-loop sidecar too. Its
# schedule is OPENWEBUI_BACKUP_INTERVAL (frontend/docker-compose.yml:375).

# GPU routing for llama-cpp and aistack services
GPU_AISTACK_DEVICE_ID=1
GPU_LLAMA_CPP_DEVICE_ID=0
GPU_LLAMA_CPP_EMBED_DEVICE_ID=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private Search Gateway (privacy-first web search over Mullvad; tor retired 2026-08-21)
# Guide: ../documentation-plans-ai-stack/implementation-guide/web-search/guide-Private-Search-Gateway.md
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED — generate long random strings (e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`):
GATEWAY_API_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
SEARXNG_SECRET_KEY=change-me-to-another-long-random-string

# Gateway behaviour (defaults are sensible; override only if needed)
PROVIDER_PRIORITY=searxng
CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=900
REQUEST_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=20
CIRCUIT_FAILURE_THRESHOLD=3
CIRCUIT_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=120
LOG_LEVEL=INFO
LOG_QUERIES=false                 # PRIVACY: keep false; true logs only a hash

# Pin images for reproducible privacy infra (replace :latest after first pull)
# Pinned 2026-09-11 (research-trust). The engine policy in
# search/searxng/settings.yml only works on this build or newer;
# on the 2026-05-17 image only yandex answers. Bump deliberately, with a
# re-measurement, never by floating :latest.
SEARXNG_IMAGE=searxng/searxng:2026.9.11-61d660276
SEARCH_REDIS_IMAGE=redis:7-alpine

# Open WebUI web-search wiring (Approach B — stock searxng engine → gateway).
ENABLE_WEB_SEARCH=True
WEB_SEARCH_ENGINE=searxng
SEARXNG_QUERY_URL=http://gateway:8080/search?q=<query>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# open-terminal — workspace plane API key (used by OWUI and, from Tool, by
# little-coder). Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OPEN_TERMINAL_API_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# little-coder — self-improving coding agent (Chapter 1: Tool)
# Docs: ../documentation-plans-ai-stack/implementation-guide/little-coder/
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pin the upstream little-coder version for reproducible builds (plan §5).
# Pin a concrete version — never `latest` (it floats; a rebuild silently jumps).
LITTLE_CODER_VERSION=1.9.7

# Route the agent's command execution into open-terminal via the bash-override
# pi extension (design §3.4). 1 = route to open-terminal (default, verified);
# 0 = built-in bash, runs in the network-isolated little-coder container.
# See little-coder/pi-extension/README.md.
LC_ROUTE_EXEC=1

# llama-cpp API key for the agent's inference (llama-swap accepts any value).
LC_LLAMA_API_KEY=llama


# Per-repo deploy token for cloning private work repos (design §10.3):
# least-privilege, HTTPS token, never the self-improvement PAT. Empty = public.
LC_DEPLOY_TOKEN=

# Backup retention for the little-coder expertise volumes. No cron variable
# exists: little-coder-backup is a sleep-loop sidecar, scheduled by
# LITTLE_CODER_BACKUP_INTERVAL (coder/docker-compose.yml:182).
LC_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2

# OPERATOR ACTION ITEMS — required before the self-improvement chapters.
# Unused in Tool; wire them now so later chapters do not retrofit the chain.
#   1. Create a PRIVATE self-improvement git remote (design §10.6).
#   2. Provision a fine-grained PAT scoped to contents:write on it ONLY —
#      no repo deletion, no admin, no other repos.
# Also: add your git remote's host to little-coder/docker/egress-allowlist.txt.
LC_SELF_REMOTE_URL=
LC_SELF_REMOTE_PAT=

# === Internet-exposed front-end (portal) ===
# See ../documentation-plans-ai-stack/implementation-guide/open-source authentication front ends for ai stack/
# Plan §8 Step 8. The portal is gated behind compose profile `internet`, so
# nothing here takes effect until `scripts/portal/portal-on.ps1` is run.

PUBLIC_DOMAIN=ai.example.com
ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Cloudflare Tunnel — required (port-forward mode removed from the plan).
# Create the tunnel in Cloudflare Zero Trust dashboard, paste token here.
CLOUDFLARE_TUNNEL_TOKEN=

# Authelia secrets — generate each with:
#   docker run --rm authelia/authelia:4.39 authelia crypto rand --length 64
AUTHELIA_JWT_SECRET=
AUTHELIA_SESSION_SECRET=
AUTHELIA_STORAGE_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# === Portal-alerter (Gmail self-send via reused OB1 OAuth client) ===
# DIGEST_TO is where alerts AND scheduled digests land. Use your own Gmail.
# DIGEST_FROM defaults to DIGEST_TO if blank; should match the Google account
# that consented to the open-brain-email OAuth client.
DIGEST_TO=
DIGEST_FROM=
DIGEST_WINDOW_HOURS=24

# Rate-limit on instant /alert emails (per rolling minute). Excess events
# in the same minute are coalesced into one summary email at minute close.
ALERT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MIN=20

# Scheduled digest cadence (cron in UTC). Default = daily 07:00 UTC.
PORTAL_DIGEST_CRON=0 7 * * *

# Integrity tripwire schedule (cron in UTC).
TRIPWIRE_CRON=0 4 * * *

# Cloudflare-tunnel liveness probe. Polls /ready endpoint and alerts
# after FAILURES_BEFORE_ALERT consecutive failures.
TUNNEL_WATCHER_POLL_SEC=30
TUNNEL_WATCHER_FAILURES_BEFORE_ALERT=3

# SurrealDB credentials (pre-flight §6.3)
SURREAL_USER=
SURREAL_PASSWORD=

# Open Notebook DB encryption key (pre-flight §6.4)
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# Backup retention for portal volumes (count of most-recent archives kept)
CADDY_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2
CADDY_BACKUP_CRON=0 3 * * *
AUTHELIA_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2
AUTHELIA_BACKUP_CRON=0 3 * * *

# === Unified snapshot — additional backup containers (Part 1 of the
# yes-its-working-now-soft-candy plan) ===
# See: documentation/runbooks/backup-conventions.md + documentation/runbooks/restore-from-snapshot.md
# Scripts: scripts/checks/check-backup-coverage.ps1, scripts/backup/restore-from-snapshot.ps1
#
# All *_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT vars are COUNTS (how many most-recent archives
# to keep), not days. Default 2 -- you also get up to 2 more copies via the
# two-slot weekly NAS rotation, for ~6 total copies per service.
#
# All backup scripts run a PRECHECK before they touch any file. They skip
# silently (and log) if the source data is empty/missing or, where
# applicable, if a TCP health probe to the source service fails. This
# prevents capturing a torn snapshot when a service is broken.

# OB1 PostgreSQL nightly logical dump (pg_dump -Fc, pgvector-preserving).
# Pull these from OB1/docker/.env -- they MUST match what openbrain-db is
# running with, else the dump fails auth.
POSTGRES_USER=postgres
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
POSTGRES_DB=openbrain
OPENBRAIN_DB_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2
OPENBRAIN_DB_BACKUP_CRON=0 2 * * *

# OB1 wiki (immutable git objects -- hot tar is safe).
OPENBRAIN_WIKI_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2

# Open Notebook: surreal export (logical) + notebook_data tar.
# Backup container connects to surrealdb as root/root by convention; see
# documentation/runbooks/restore-from-snapshot.md.
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_BACKUP_CRON=20 2 * * *

TAILSCALE_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2

# GGUF model store (~110GB at present; the .lmstudio path is historical).
# Weekly cadence so two NAS slots = ~220GB max. Set LM_MODELS_BACKUP_INTERVAL=
# (empty) to DISABLE this backup if NAS storage becomes the bottleneck --
# lm-models-backup then logs DISABLED and exits
# (inference/compose/backups.yml:41, 75, 82-84 — the inference plane split into
# included service groups 2026-09-19).
LM_MODELS_BACKUP_RETAIN_COUNT=2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LiteLLM gateway — transparent interposition (guide §1A, added 2026-06-12)
# Docs: ../documentation-plans-ai-stack/implementation-guide/LiteLLM-Proxy/guide-LiteLLM-Proxy.md
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Internal Postgres password for llm-gateway-db (reachable only on the internal
# llm-net). Generate: a long random hex string. Real value lives in .env (gitignored).
LITELLM_DB_PASSWORD=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
# Spend-log backup retention (non-authoritative telemetry DB). No schedule
# variable exists: llm-gateway-backup loops on a hardcoded `sleep 86400` in its
# entrypoint (inference/compose/backups.yml:13,31).
LITELLM_BACKUP_RETAIN_DAYS=7
# OpenRouter key for the gateway's CLOUD model group
# (inference/config/litellm/model_list/cloud.openrouter.yaml). BLANK = those
# models are not registered at all and nothing about the gateway changes -
# that is the shipped state. Read that file before setting it: llm-gateway
# sits on two internal:true networks and has NO egress today, and agent-org
# OD-6 puts the cloud lane on its own separate gateway. Setting a key here
# makes the models LISTED, not reachable.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# NOTE: the gateway ENFORCES per-caller virtual keys since the J.1 flip
# (2026-08-21): `master_key: os.environ/LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` under
# general_settings in inference/config/litellm.config.yaml. LITELLM_MASTER_KEY
# is REQUIRED — leaving it blank is a 401 for every caller. Set it in the
# REQUIRED-secrets block below and issue per-caller sk- keys via
# llm-gateway-ui /key/generate.
# ============================================================================
# Added 2026-08-20 (CLEANUP-PLAN v3 A.4): the 58 compose-referenced vars that
# were missing from this template. Secrets are blank on purpose. Vars with a
# compose-side default (${VAR:-default}) list that default here.
# ============================================================================

# --- REQUIRED secrets (blank = fill in) -------------------------------------
# J.1 (2026-08-21): LiteLLM master key + per-caller virtual keys. Issue caller
# keys via llm-gateway-ui /key/generate (see LiteLLM-Proxy/J1-VIRTUAL-KEYS-
# CUTOVER.md). Every inference consumer needs its key or gets 401.
LITELLM_MASTER_KEY=
MNEMORY_LLM_API_KEY=
LC_LLAMA_API_KEY=
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_LLM_API_KEY=
# Encrypts values at rest in webui.db. Pin once; a rotate breaks every
# encrypted credential + all sessions. (Placeholder value on purpose: this
# var carries a ${...:?} fail-loud guard in frontend/docker-compose.yml, and
# an EMPTY value trips it — which would break the CI compose validation.)
WEBUI_SECRET_KEY=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
# OWUI's own LiteLLM virtual key (J.1) — also injected into the openwebui
# container for the status-pipe queue board (frontend/docker-compose.yml).
OWUI_CHAT_LLM_API_KEY=sk-change-me-owui-virtual-key
# Mullvad WireGuard (search-plane engine egress). Placeholder for the same
# reason: the ${...:?} guard in search/docker-compose.yml rejects empty.
MULLVAD_WG_PRIVATE_KEY=change-me-real-wg-private-key
MULLVAD_WG_ADDRESSES=
# LiteLLM Admin-UI sidecar (llm-gateway-ui, tailnet :8445/ui)
LITELLM_UI_MASTER_KEY=
LITELLM_UI_USERNAME=
LITELLM_UI_PASSWORD=
# mnemory privacy gateway (:8060)
MNEMORY_GATEWAY_KEY=
MNEMORY_CLOUD_USER=
# Quartz workbench write API key
WORKBENCH_KEY=

# --- Search plane ------------------------------------------------------------
MULLVAD_COUNTRIES=Netherlands
VPN_IMAGE=qmcgaw/gluetun:latest
SEARCH_NET_SUBNET=172.16.0.0/12

# --- OpenWebUI feature flags --------------------------------------------------
ENABLE_CODE_INTERPRETER=False
ENABLE_CODE_EXECUTION=False

# --- llm-queue (B2 admission controller) --------------------------------------
LLM_QUEUE_SLOTS=3
LLM_QUEUE_MAX_IN_FLIGHT=4
LLM_QUEUE_ENFORCE_BUDGET=true
LLM_QUEUE_EVENTS_DB_PATH=/data/events.db
LLM_QUEUE_BACKSTOP_DEPTH=24
LLM_QUEUE_LOG_LEVEL=INFO
LLM_QUEUE_MAX_TOTAL_CONNECTIONS=128
LLM_QUEUE_T_INITIAL_S=30
LLM_QUEUE_T_WINDOW=5
LLM_QUEUE_UPSTREAM_TIMEOUT_S=600

# --- llama.cpp serve routes (tailscale entrypoint) -----------------------------
LLAMA_CPP_ENABLED=true
LLAMA_CPP_HOST=llama-cpp
LLAMA_CPP_PORT=8080
LLAMA_CPP_EMBED_ENABLED=true
LLAMA_CPP_EMBED_HOST=llama-cpp-embed
LLAMA_CPP_EMBED_PORT=8080
LLAMA_ARG_CTX_CHECKPOINTS=8

# --- llama-swap speculative decoding (qwen36-27b MTP) ---------------------------
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_SPEC_TYPE=draft-mtp
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_SPEC_DRAFT_N_MAX=8
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_SPEC_DRAFT_CACHE_TYPE_K=q8_0
LLAMA_SWAP_QWEN36_27B_SPEC_DRAFT_CACHE_TYPE_V=q8_0

# --- open_notebook / Quartz serve routes ---------------------------------------
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_ENABLED=true
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_HOST=open_notebook
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_PORT=8502
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_TS_PORT=8443
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_API_PORT=5055
OPEN_NOTEBOOK_API_TS_PORT=5055
QUARTZ_ENABLED=true
QUARTZ_HOST=caddy
QUARTZ_PORT=8446
QUARTZ_TS_PORT=8444

# --- Backup sidecar knobs (generic-tar sidecars unless noted) --------------------
BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
RETAIN_COUNT=2
MIN_AGE_SECS=82800
TAILSCALE_BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
MNEMORY_BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
OPENWEBUI_BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
LITTLE_CODER_BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
LM_MODELS_BACKUP_INTERVAL=604800
LM_MODELS_BACKUP_MIN_AGE_SECS=43200
LM_MODELS_BACKUP_HEALTH_TCP=llama-cpp-upstream:8080
OPENBRAIN_WIKI_BACKUP_INTERVAL=86400
CADDY_BACKUP_HEALTH_TCP=caddy:80
AUTHELIA_BACKUP_HEALTH_TCP=authelia:9091

# --- M.8 T2 validation profile ---
# Real value lives in gitignored .env.test (created via the J.1 /key/generate
# flow, alias test-validation): the low-priority gateway lane for ephemeral
# test twins (issue_ops.py t2). Copy this line there with the minted key:
TEST_VALIDATION_LLM_KEY=sk-change-me-test-validation

# --- NAS off-site backup credentials (2026-09-13) -------------------------
# The dedicated Synology backup-only SMB user used by the WEEKLY off-site
# sync, scripts/backup/backup-to-nas.ps1 (scheduled task 'AI-Stack Portal
# NAS Backup'). Real values live in the gitignored .env.
#
# Synology EXPIRES this password on a schedule. When it does, `net use`
# fails with system error 2242 and the weekly sync stops - it did exactly
# that between 2026-08-30 and 2026-09-13, missing two runs. To rotate:
#   1. set the new password on the NAS (DSM > Control Panel > User)
#   2. put it in .env as NAS_BACKUP_PASSWORD
#   3. powershell -File scripts/backup/set-nas-credential.ps1 -FromEnv
#      (re-seals secrets/nas-backup-vault.dat, the DPAPI fallback store)
# The script reads .env FIRST and the vault only as a fallback, so step 3
# is for keeping the two in step - not required for the next run to work.
NAS_BACKUP_USER=backup-user
NAS_BACKUP_PASSWORD=change-me-see-comment-above
